---
// Docs view of the npm route: nothing until npm has the app release's version,
// then install, first source, and harness connection with the same commands
// /install shows (src/data/npm-cli.mjs).
import { Code } from '@astrojs/starlight/components';
import { appTag } from '../data/app-release';
import { npmCli, WSL_MANIFEST_NOTE } from '../data/npm-release';
---
{npmCli && (
	<>
		<h2 id="cli-without-the-app">Use the CLI without the app</h2>
		<p>
			The <code>contextcake</code> command runs on {npmCli.platforms} with Node.js 22 or newer.
			Windows works on a best-effort basis. Install the version that matches {appTag}:
		</p>
		<Code lang="bash" code={npmCli.install} />
		<p>
			npm installs the tarball attached to the {appTag} release, with registry integrity <code>{npmCli.tarballIntegrity}</code>. The package runs no install scripts.
		</p>
		<p>Create a manifest, then add a folder of Markdown files as your first source:</p>
		<Code lang="bash" code={npmCli.setup} />
		<p>
			<code>init</code> leaves an existing manifest alone. On macOS the CLI and the app read the same
			manifest. {WSL_MANIFEST_NOTE}
		</p>
		<p>
			Connect an AI tool with the absolute path of the install you just made. Check which <code>contextcake</code>{' '}
			your shell finds. The connect command saves that full path.
		</p>
		<Code lang="bash" code={`${npmCli.resolve}\n${npmCli.connectClaude}`} title="Claude Code" />
		<Code lang="bash" code={npmCli.connectCodex} title="Codex" />
		<p>
			To try a command without installing, run <code>{npmCli.npx}</code>. Keep npx out of AI tool
			settings: it runs from a cache folder that can change.
		</p>
	</>
)}
